
+ For all the ID types, start using [str; 32] or the closest approximation
  (maybe [u8; 32]) instead of String. Non-copy types suck here.

+ Finish implementing the server.
    + TakeTurn should be implemented and (if successful) notify all participants
      of a room that the change occurred, *and what it was* (not just the new state).
    + CreateRoom should notify all sockets of a player that the room was
      created.
    + The server should delete any socket that hasn't had activity in, say, an hour.

+ Generalize the server.
    + Parameterize Server over Game and Turn types.
    + Create the Game trait.
    + Separate Server out into its own crate.

+ Finish implementing the game.
    + In cell.rs, make a Stone type instead of having it be a tuple in Cell.
    + Implement the "who plays black" thing as a game setting.

+ Write tests for the web app?
    - Host can't play until player arrives.
    - Frame all tests in terms of the actions of *clients* (i.e. WebSocket connections).
      These clients can do things like create accounts, take actions with those accounts
      (by providing the API-given credential), and maybe other things? But ultimately
      they are the things that the server deals with.
    - Same user can have multiple clients and they all work.
+ Make there be a 1/100 chance that a stone is textured with the stare emoji (not in the first ~5 moves so as not to be confusing).

+ Write a bunch of insta tests.
    - Wins.
